    "product_id": "94d756c6-6641-4427-85b8-9a3ae1865f84",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 171129\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n429 AM PDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Isolated showers and thunderstorms possible in eastern Lincoln \n  County early this morning. Otherwise, expect drying conditions \n  through the weekend.\n\n* Chances of showers and thunderstorms return early next week;  \n  temperatures climb back to above-normal midweek.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Today through Wednesday.\n\nMany thunderstorms produced moderate-to-heavy rainfall yesterday, \nwith several gauges measuring between 1.50 and 2.00 inches of \nrainfall in under 2 hours - particularly across southern Mohave \nCounty. CAMs are persistent in isolated convection returning to \neastern Lincoln County early this morning between 3 to 6 AM. \nThough shear remains low in this region, PWATs remain around 1.00 \ninches with over 1200 J/kg of SBCAPE. As a result, while storms \nwill likely remain sub-severe, they will have the potential to \ncreate lightning and light-to-moderate rainfall. Otherwise, a \nclosed area of low pressure will continue to traverse eastward \nacross the southern Great Basin today, which will once again \nelevate south-southwesterly winds in our forecast area. This \nclosed low will usher dry air into the region, allowing PWATs to \ndrop back below 0.50 inches. Temperatures will remain near-normal \nthrough the weekend.\n\nEarly next week, ensemble means indicate a resurgence of PWATs in \nexcess of 1.00 inches, which will coincide with the approach of a \nclosed area of low pressure pushing into the central California \nCoast from the eastern Pacific Ocean. This low will provide the \ninstability necessary to bring a return of PoPs to the region, \nwith best chances across the Mojave Desert. Mid-week, ridging \nreturns to the Desert Southwest, which will allow temperatures to \nclimb back to above-normal.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 12Z Forecast Package...Light\nsoutheasterly winds will become elevated and gusty by mid- \nmorning, with gusts to 15-20KT expected before veering to the \nsouth-southwest by early afternoon. Thereafter through sunset, \nwinds will gust to around 25KT, with winds and gusts diminishing \nthrough the evening. Another round of gusty southerly winds is \nexpected at the end of the forecast period. VFR conditions will \nprevail with FEW-SCT clouds with bases around 10kft through this \nafternoon.\n\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 12Z Forecast Package...Another round of gusty\nsoutheasterly winds is expected to develop later this morning, \nbefore winds veer to the south and southwest by this afternoon. \nGusts to 20-25KT will be common across the region through sunset, \nwith slightly stronger gusts to around 30KT possible in the \nnorthern Owens Valley where up-valley winds will develop. \nAdditionally, winds across the western Mojave Desert will remain \naround 10KT or less until late this afternoon, when westerly gusts\nto around 25KT will develop and persist into tonight. Showers and\nthunderstorms are expected, mainly this morning and early \nafternoon over northern Mohave and southeastern Lincoln counties, \nwith direct impacts not expected at area terminals. FEW-SCT \nclouds with bases at or above 10kft are expected through this \nafternoon for most of the region, with VFR conditions prevailing.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Soulat \nAVIATION...Phillipson\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
